child the boy died at the seam and the  dad died in hospital later  with a toxic drug crisis gripping Canada  there are calls for more action and  renewed urgency on this International  overdose Awareness Day Advocates friends  and families gathered in cities across  Canada to honor the lives of those lost  to overdose educational demonstrations  and conversations happening today the  aim is to bring awareness to the crisis  and to reduce the stigma associated with  substance use this year many provinces  are on track for Grim new records for  overdose deaths  and after a long wait Canada's Telecom  regulator has approved a new three-digit  Suicide Prevention and Mental Health  crisis hotline the crtc says Canadians  who need urgent help during a mental  health crisis will be able to call or  text 988 free of charge Nationwide  starting in November of 2023. those are  the same three digits adopted by a  similar hotline launched in the United  States last month the announcement comes  more than 19 months after the House of  Commons unanimously voted in favor of  establishing the hotline  the employer Strikes Back ahead what  quiet firing is and why workers need to  watch out  the Bank of Canada could move forward  with another aggressive interest rate  hike next week new data shows the  economy stayed in relatively good shape  during the second quarter of the year  the economy grew at an annual rate of  3.3 percent from April to June now  that's still short of the bank of  Canada's forecast businesses ramped up  their investment in inventory and there  was a spike in household spending on  clothing and Footwear as more people  head back to the office  but not everyone is Keen to part with  their work from home setup some are even  sticking it to employers through the  quiet quitting movement doing your job  to the requirements but nothing above  and beyond well now there's another new  labor Trend emerging and it's called  quiet firing and as Ann gaviola explains  it's gaining momentum  and usually that person doesn't just get  extremely underpaid they're also treated  really badly in the workplace quiet  quitting meet quiet firing which is also  sparking conversations on Tick Tock and  Beyond it's almost like a game of  chicken sometimes for employees and  employers quiet firing involves  withholding promotions and important  work in the hopes of pushing an employee  out no firing involved but it can lead  to legal and financial consequences for  a company that doesn't tread carefully  if you are mistreating an employee and  making their lives miserable within the  workplace you could be held liable for  committing a constructive dismissal  quiet quitting which is just doing your  job and nothing more is a symptom of a  Workforce that feels overworked and  burnt out after the roller coaster of  the last two years some setting clear  boundaries with the lines between work  and home life were blurred Economist  Linda Nazareth warns managers and  employers that quiet firing is a symptom  of something else too honestly if you  are quietly firing people it's either a  bad Management in that you're trying to  not pay them in a layoff or you just  don't want to deal with this head-on and  now employees have little tolerance for  poor leadership with many workplaces  still in flux some bosses are having a  hard time adjusting you have managers  who are being asked to manage hybrid and  remote workforces they weren't trained  to do that and they're also supposed to  engage a Workforce who may be present  maybe somewhere else but who does not  feel that they are particularly happy  with things quiet firing can poison  workplace culture and in this  competitive labor market quiet firing  can mean losing your top talent too and  they're not afraid to call out quiet  firing on the way out after all loud  quitting has never been easier what is  loudly quitting you under packing your  belongings and leaving you don't pay me  my worth someone else will  and gaviola Global News Toronto  remembering Diana next how the Royals  tragic death is still sending shock  waves 25 years later  many people remember where they were 25  years ago today when they heard the news  that Princess Diana had died she was one  of the most famous women of the 20th  century and her tragic death at the age  of 36 sent shockwaves Around the World  Eric Sorensen has more on her life her  Legacy and her lasting impact on the  monarchy  it doesn't seem so long ago for many the  memory of Princess Diana has not faded  the sadness has not eased the day  I'm about it was awful just like  yesterday  the chance you approach you right back  to those days 25 years ago the heartache  was as deep as the ocean of bouquets  outside Kensington Palace  Public's first glimpses  the shy princess to be and I suppose in  love  gross  the wedding of the century  [Music]  and a mother of a future Monarch Diana  was one of the most compelling figures  in the 20th century  so charismatic she out Sean Charles and  the royal family but her husband's  Affairs and then her own brought a very  public and agonizing end to the marriage  did nothing to diminish The public's  fascination with Diana she was glamorous  and a global icon who still captivated  the public with her causes like advocacy  and support of those with HIV AIDS and  her Embrace of those in need of help  and then in an instant  she was gone the public outpouring was  unlike anything we've seen before was  down in the queen of people's house just  look at the evidence the fact that  people mourned her loss probably in some  ways greater than their members of their  own family Diana left her Mark the royal  family was forced to respond to public  scrutiny and in turn learned to reach  out Diana probably challenged the royal  family the way it operates in a way that  no one had ever done before and that no  one not even Harry has been able to do  since Diana's enduring Legacy of course  is her children in Prince Harry her  rebelliousness an outsider in the royal  family I wanted to be a day to share the  spirit of my mum with my family with my  children who I wish could have met her  and William who is to be king is 40 now  he dotes on his children and he has her  natural ease meeting Ordinary People it  was Diana's way of bringing up her boys  that is the influence on the money of  the future in Paris where she died they  recall a generosity of spirit  lady Dai worked for social causes he  says and United all Races I think it's  just someone who needs to be remembered  remembered forever and for other things  she did Diana will be remembered as a  princess with a common touch  Eric Sorensen Global News  it's that last line of the Elton John  song that comes to me your candle burned  out long before your Legend ever did  that's global national for this  Wednesday night I'm Farah Nasser thank  you for spending part of your evening  with us tonight's your Canada is  Kootenai National Park in the  Southeastern British Columbia until  tomorrow take care of yourselves and  each other good night  [Music]
 

